#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/pinning/definition-of-pin.md:6
msgid "Definition of Pin"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/pinning/definition-of-pin.md:9
msgid "// Copyright 2026 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/pinning/definition-of-pin.md:10
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/pinning/definition-of-pin.md:29
msgid ""
"`Pin` is a minimal wrapper around a _pointer type_, which is defined as a "
"type that implements `Deref`."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/pinning/definition-of-pin.md:32
msgid ""
"However, `Pin::new()` only accepts types that dereference into a target that "
"implements `Unpin` (`Deref<Target: Unpin>`). This allows `Pin` to rely on "
"the type system to enforce its guarantees."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/pinning/definition-of-pin.md:36
msgid ""
"Types that do not implement `Unpin`, i.e., types that require pinning, must "
"create a `Pin` via the unsafe `Pin::new_unchecked()`."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/pinning/definition-of-pin.md:39
msgid ""
"Aside: Unlike other `new()`/`new_unchecked()` method pairs, `new` does not "
"do any runtime checking. The check is a zero-cost compile-time check."
msgstr ""
